WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.602 --> 00:00:05.238
Therapy helps us
understand our emotions

00:00:05.271 --> 00:00:08.141
and there's a lot of evidence
to suggest that it can help us

00:00:08.174 --> 00:00:10.777
improve our functionality
and quality of life.

00:00:10.810 --> 00:00:14.147
62% of patients
with Major Depressive Disorder

00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:16.249
showed improvement
after psychotherapy.

00:00:16.282 --> 00:00:18.785
<i> But people's views
 on therapy are mixed.</i>

00:00:18.818 --> 00:00:21.587
<i> Some people believe
 that therapy isn't for them.</i>

00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:23.723 line:0%
In Latino culture,
that's just not something

00:00:23.756 --> 00:00:27.326 line:0%
that's readily talked about
or on the table.

00:00:27.359 --> 00:00:29.162 line:0%
They were just like,
"You need to replace

00:00:29.195 --> 00:00:30.663 line:0%
your thoughts
with positive thoughts,"

00:00:30.696 --> 00:00:32.165 line:0%
and that's not helpful.

00:00:32.198 --> 00:00:33.566 line:0%
Fabiola:
<i> While others say...</i>

00:00:33.599 --> 00:00:35.168 line:0%
Therapy has helped me
to be a better writer

00:00:35.201 --> 00:00:36.502 line:0%
and a better lover,

00:00:36.535 --> 00:00:38.504 line:0%
a better son,
all those things.

00:00:38.537 --> 00:00:40.406 line:0%
It's literally been
life-changing for me.

00:00:40.439 --> 00:00:43.509 line:0%
One important thing
about going to therapy

00:00:43.542 --> 00:00:47.180 line:0%
is realizing that
you are in control of
your own narrative.

00:00:47.213 --> 00:00:51.217
I look at therapy
like I'm walking onto
a construction site

00:00:51.250 --> 00:00:54.353
and I am the product
that we're building.

00:00:54.386 --> 00:00:57.523
Everyone's experience
with therapy is different,

00:00:57.556 --> 00:01:01.160
and whether someone finds
value in it can be subjective.

00:01:01.193 --> 00:01:04.363
<i> But if you look closer,
 we might even realize</i>

00:01:04.396 --> 00:01:06.532
<i> that our views on therapy
 are limited,</i>

00:01:06.565 --> 00:01:09.535
<i> since they so often
 center psychotherapy</i>

00:01:09.568 --> 00:01:12.305
<i> and leave out a much bigger
 world of healing.</i>

00:01:12.338 --> 00:01:16.209
Therapy was created
from a Euro-centric lens

00:01:16.242 --> 00:01:18.845
for individuals that come from
a Euro-centric background.

00:01:18.878 --> 00:01:21.547
In a year that was
especially challenging,

00:01:21.580 --> 00:01:24.884
the mental health conversation
is more important than ever,

00:01:24.917 --> 00:01:28.154
and that got me thinking,
is therapy for everyone?

00:01:30.923 --> 00:01:34.260 line:0%
<i> ( theme music playing )</i>

00:01:38.797 --> 00:01:40.633
- Hey, y'all.
- Both: Hey.

00:01:40.666 --> 00:01:44.370 align:start position:0% line:0%
So, therapy,
was that ever something
that crossed your mind?

00:01:44.403 --> 00:01:45.905 align:start size:69% position:31%
I've never really
thought about therapy.

00:01:45.938 --> 00:01:48.441 align:start size:66% position:34%
I think I always
thought of therapy

00:01:48.474 --> 00:01:52.445 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
as you pay someone
to listen to you talk
about yourself for an hour.

00:01:52.478 --> 00:01:55.748
It didn't really
cross my mind that even
though probably I had

00:01:55.781 --> 00:01:58.551
some of the hardest times
ever this year,

00:01:58.584 --> 00:02:00.920
that I should go that route.

00:02:00.953 --> 00:02:02.588 align:start position:0%
For me,
talking to a therapist,

00:02:02.621 --> 00:02:04.557 align:start position:0%
just having someone
to share things

00:02:04.590 --> 00:02:07.260 align:start position:0% line:0%
that I don't feel comfortable
sharing with my partner,

00:02:07.293 --> 00:02:10.763 align:start position:0% line:0%
my family, and having someone
who's non-judgmental

00:02:10.796 --> 00:02:13.900 align:start position:0%
is something
that really has fundamentally
changed a lot for me.

00:02:13.933 --> 00:02:16.602 align:start position:0%
The number of people
dealing with anxiety

00:02:16.635 --> 00:02:19.272 align:start position:0%
and also suicide
during the pandemic
has skyrocketed,

00:02:19.305 --> 00:02:21.841 align:start position:0%
and we know that therapy
is highly effective for that.

00:02:21.874 --> 00:02:25.611
For severe anxiety,
in January of 2020,

00:02:25.644 --> 00:02:29.615
we were at 71% of people
reporting severe anxiety.

00:02:29.648 --> 00:02:31.918
And then by September
it was 80%.

00:02:31.951 --> 00:02:37.456
Also, the number of people
reporting frequent
suicidal ideation increased,

00:02:37.489 --> 00:02:42.461 align:start position:0%
but the problem is that
a lot of people just are
not accessing therapy.

00:02:42.494 --> 00:02:46.732 align:start position:0%
I have a report here from
the city's mental health arm.

00:02:46.765 --> 00:02:49.635 align:start position:0%
We see that there's
clear disparity
along racial lines

00:02:49.668 --> 00:02:52.371 align:start position:0%
of who's accessing therapy
and who's not.

00:02:52.404 --> 00:02:55.708 align:start size:91% position:9%
58% of depressed white people
are receiving therapy,

00:02:55.741 --> 00:02:58.644 align:start size:78% position:22%
whereas only 30%
of depressed black people

00:02:58.677 --> 00:02:59.779 size:84% position:58%
are getting that treatment.

00:02:59.812 --> 00:03:01.948 align:start size:75% position:25%
- Fabiola: Yeah.
- Lee: Wow.

00:03:01.981 --> 00:03:03.983 align:start position:0%
And so my question is,
in a city like New York City,

00:03:04.016 --> 00:03:07.954 align:start position:0%
why is it still that just
white people are getting therapy

00:03:07.987 --> 00:03:10.590 align:start position:0%
in a way that their black
and brown counterparts aren't?

00:03:10.623 --> 00:03:13.993 align:start size:78% position:22%
If I had to guess,
it would be access to it.

00:03:14.026 --> 00:03:18.497 line:0%
On my worst days, that's when
I'm like, "Okay, I need help."

00:03:18.530 --> 00:03:21.801 line:0%
And then I start looking
for help, and then I realize
I can't afford help.

00:03:21.834 --> 00:03:24.337
And then, even if they do
take your insurance,

00:03:24.370 --> 00:03:26.706
their office
might not be accessible.

00:03:26.739 --> 00:03:29.675
On top of that,
your therapist might be ableist.

00:03:29.708 --> 00:03:31.844
But not a lot of them speak

00:03:31.877 --> 00:03:33.512
a lot of our native tongues,
you know?

00:03:33.545 --> 00:03:35.848
Like, a lot of them
operate in English.

00:03:35.881 --> 00:03:39.352 line:0%
It's really all about
what will society say?

00:03:39.385 --> 00:03:40.987 line:0%
 The classic, like,
<i> "log kya kahenge,"</i>

00:03:41.020 --> 00:03:42.889 line:0%
like, what's everyone else
gonna say?

00:03:45.991 --> 00:03:49.629 align:start position:0%
So, I want to talk to you
because I know that one in five

00:03:49.662 --> 00:03:51.764 align:start position:0%
Americans lives
with a mental illness.

00:03:51.797 --> 00:03:55.701 align:start position:0% line:0%
But about 60% of people
aren't receiving the care
that they need.

00:03:55.734 --> 00:03:58.437 align:start position:0% line:0%
How come so many people
aren't accessing therapy?

00:03:58.470 --> 00:04:03.509 align:start size:72% position:28%
So, a main one,
not the only, is money.

00:04:03.542 --> 00:04:07.380 size:88% position:56%
So, you're thinking anywhere
between $75 at the low end.

00:04:07.413 --> 00:04:09.649 align:start size:78% position:22%
I've seen as high as $600
an hour at the high end.

00:04:09.682 --> 00:04:12.585 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Wow.
- Let's take Patient A.

00:04:12.618 --> 00:04:15.388 align:start size:72% position:28%
Patient A is struggling
with symptoms

00:04:15.421 --> 00:04:17.556 align:start size:81% position:19%
that we typically would
associate with depression.

00:04:17.589 --> 00:04:20.026 size:84% position:58%
She really would love
to go see somebody,

00:04:20.059 --> 00:04:23.396 align:start size:75% position:25%
but her primary struggle
is how to pay for it.

00:04:23.429 --> 00:04:26.832 align:start size:78% position:22%
What we know from a study
by the National Council
on Behavioral Health

00:04:26.865 --> 00:04:32.338 align:start size:63% position:38% line:0%
is that 42% of folks
indicate that money

00:04:32.371 --> 00:04:35.908 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
is a primary reason
why it makes it difficult
to access mental healthcare.

00:04:35.941 --> 00:04:38.010 size:78% position:39%
In my journey with trying
to seek out a therapist,

00:04:38.043 --> 00:04:39.412
it's always this question of,

00:04:39.445 --> 00:04:41.981 align:start position:0%
"Will my insurance
cover this therapist?"

00:04:42.014 --> 00:04:45.751
But my therapists just always
seem to be out of network.

00:04:45.784 --> 00:04:47.586 align:start size:91% position:9%
- So, why is that?
- There's a couple things.

00:04:47.619 --> 00:04:49.055 align:start size:81% position:19%
One is,
many don't take insurance,

00:04:49.088 --> 00:04:50.456 align:start size:66% position:34%
and for those who do,

00:04:50.489 --> 00:04:52.058 align:start size:75% position:25%
they're limited
in terms of the numbers

00:04:52.091 --> 00:04:54.327 align:start size:66% position:34%
of types of insurance
that they take.

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:58.064 align:start size:69% position:31%
Part of the reason
is this lack of parity

00:04:58.097 --> 00:05:01.701 align:start size:84% position:16%
between what you can charge
and be reimbursed for

00:05:01.734 --> 00:05:03.436 align:start size:63% position:38%
as a mental
healthcare provider.

00:05:03.469 --> 00:05:06.439 size:84% position:58%
There are many providers
who struggle with this idea

00:05:06.472 --> 00:05:08.607 align:start size:63% position:38%
of how much
they get reimbursed.

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:11.077 align:start size:91% position:9%
And so what ends up happening
with a lot of providers

00:05:11.110 --> 00:05:13.346 align:start size:63% position:38%
is they just decide,
"I won't take
insurance at all."

00:05:13.379 --> 00:05:16.549 align:start position:0%
So, who is Patient B?
What's going on with Patient B?

00:05:16.582 --> 00:05:19.785 align:start size:94% position:6%
So, she has money
or she has the right insurance
to be able to pay,

00:05:19.818 --> 00:05:22.455 align:start size:75% position:25%
but there is a shortage.

00:05:22.488 --> 00:05:27.460 align:start size:66% position:34% line:0%
Research tell us that
about 35% of people
in the United States

00:05:27.493 --> 00:05:32.765 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
live in areas where
they don't have access
to mental health providers. 

00:05:32.798 --> 00:05:36.469 align:start size:75% position:25%
For every 100,000 people
in a rural area,

00:05:36.502 --> 00:05:38.971 align:start size:59% position:41%
there are only
nine psychologists.

00:05:39.004 --> 00:05:40.973 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Wow. Yeah.
- Which is mind-blowing.

00:05:41.006 --> 00:05:44.710 align:start size:78% position:22%
Versus urban areas,
for every 100,000 people,

00:05:44.743 --> 00:05:46.712 size:84% position:58%
there are 33 psychologists.

00:05:46.745 --> 00:05:48.781 align:start size:78% position:22%
To be a little bit crass,

00:05:48.814 --> 00:05:50.483 align:start size:72% position:28%
going into
the mental health field

00:05:50.516 --> 00:05:51.884 align:start size:81% position:19%
is not as sexy as going
into some of these others.

00:05:51.917 --> 00:05:53.786 align:start size:78% position:22%
People who go
into mental health fields

00:05:53.819 --> 00:05:55.421 align:start size:88% position:13%
are sort of at the lower end
of the food chain

00:05:55.454 --> 00:05:56.689 align:start size:81% position:19%
in terms of how much money
they're gonna make.

00:05:56.722 --> 00:05:58.724 align:start position:0%
So, who is Patient C?

00:05:58.757 --> 00:06:01.627 align:start size:91% position:9%
Patient C is
an Asian-American young woman

00:06:01.660 --> 00:06:05.398 align:start size:75% position:25%
who also identifies
as a member of the queer
LGBTQ community.

00:06:05.431 --> 00:06:08.401 align:start size:81% position:19%
The trouble is not only
availability of providers,

00:06:08.434 --> 00:06:11.971 align:start size:63% position:38%
but providers'
cultural competence.

00:06:12.004 --> 00:06:13.839 align:start size:63% position:38%
Because she has
multiple identities,

00:06:13.872 --> 00:06:16.108 align:start size:66% position:34%
which we all know now
as intersectionality,

00:06:16.141 --> 00:06:20.413 align:start size:81% position:19%
the provider
that she has access to
is not necessarily someone

00:06:20.446 --> 00:06:23.048 align:start size:81% position:19%
who understands all facets
of her identity.

00:06:23.081 --> 00:06:27.153 align:start size:63% position:38%
86% of psychologists

00:06:27.186 --> 00:06:30.189 align:start size:63% position:38%
in the United States
identify as white.

00:06:30.222 --> 00:06:32.925 align:start size:94% position:6%
- That's jarring to me.
- If you're a white provider

00:06:32.958 --> 00:06:35.628 align:start size:53% position:47%
or, you know,
have any identity
of privilege,

00:06:35.661 --> 00:06:38.831 align:start size:81% position:19%
the last thing
you want to do is get
your patient to teach you.

00:06:38.864 --> 00:06:40.866 align:start size:66% position:34%
You should be trying
to learn on your own.

00:06:40.899 --> 00:06:43.702 align:start position:0%
So now we are here
at Patient D.

00:06:43.735 --> 00:06:45.171 align:start position:0%
What's going on
with Patient D?

00:06:45.204 --> 00:06:48.207 align:start size:91% position:9%
So, Patient D is
an African-American black man

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:51.577
struggling with and managing
Post Traumatic Stress Disorder.

00:06:51.610 --> 00:06:55.781 align:start size:81% position:19%
There's always a hierarchy
of healthcare concerns.

00:06:55.814 --> 00:06:59.185 align:start size:91% position:9%
Unfortunately,
mental illness being probably
the most stigmatized.

00:06:59.218 --> 00:07:04.623 align:start size:81% position:19% line:0%
A CBS poll found that
51% of the people surveyed

00:07:04.656 --> 00:07:10.563 line:0%
said that stigma was an issue
negatively impacting people

00:07:10.596 --> 00:07:12.731 align:start size:84% position:16%
who were struggling
with their mental health,
people with mental illness.

00:07:12.764 --> 00:07:16.902 align:start size:81% position:19%
How the world views people
with mental illness,

00:07:16.935 --> 00:07:18.170 align:start size:63% position:38%
more often than not,
is not positive.

00:07:18.203 --> 00:07:20.039 align:start position:0%
Where does
the stigma come from?

00:07:20.072 --> 00:07:23.209 align:start size:84% position:16%
So, stigma really has to do
with the perceptions

00:07:23.242 --> 00:07:26.512 align:start size:78% position:22%
that we hold,
and it impacts all of us.

00:07:26.545 --> 00:07:28.581 align:start size:56% position:44%
I think more so,
though, it impacts
how black people

00:07:28.614 --> 00:07:30.749 align:start size:84% position:16%
are treated in relationship
to mental illness.

00:07:30.782 --> 00:07:33.919 align:start size:97% position:3%
They're not met with open arms,
"Come, let us help you."

00:07:33.952 --> 00:07:37.022 size:84% position:58%
They are literally met with
life or death situations.

00:07:37.055 --> 00:07:38.757 align:start size:78% position:22%
So, to understand stigma,

00:07:38.790 --> 00:07:40.460 align:start size:63% position:38%
you really have to
look at the history.

00:07:44.530 --> 00:07:46.098
Fabiola:
<i> Many communities
 battle stigma</i>

00:07:46.131 --> 00:07:48.767
<i> around therapy
 and mental illness.</i>

00:07:48.800 --> 00:07:51.103
<i> Within the black community,
 the stigma rises</i>

00:07:51.136 --> 00:07:53.606
<i> from a long
 and often dark history</i>

00:07:53.639 --> 00:07:56.242
<i> of how America has dealt
 with mental health.</i>

00:07:56.275 --> 00:07:58.244
<i> And I curated this exhibit</i>

00:07:58.277 --> 00:08:00.212
<i> to take us through
 that troubled history.</i>

00:08:00.245 --> 00:08:01.614
- Thank you for coming.
- Christophe: Thank you.

00:08:01.647 --> 00:08:02.848 align:start position:0%
Here are your headphones
for the exhibit.

00:08:02.881 --> 00:08:04.583 align:start size:38% position:63%
Cleo: Thanks

00:08:04.616 --> 00:08:07.553
- I'll see you at the end.
- Christophe: Sounds good.

00:08:07.586 --> 00:08:10.122
Fabiola:
<i> Let's start at the beginning.</i>

00:08:10.155 --> 00:08:14.593
<i> For much of the 19th
 century, people living
 with mental illnesses</i>

00:08:14.626 --> 00:08:17.263
<i> were isolated
 in insane asylums.</i>

00:08:17.296 --> 00:08:21.267
<i> For the few black patients,
 they were segregated
 from white people.</i>

00:08:21.300 --> 00:08:23.269
<i> They were forced
 to labor in the fields,</i>

00:08:23.302 --> 00:08:27.806
<i> conditions not very different
 from life on plantations.</i>

00:08:27.839 --> 00:08:30.209 line:0%
<i> The other black mentally ill
 patients were sent to jail</i>

00:08:30.242 --> 00:08:31.210 line:0%
<i> or mental hospitals.</i>

00:08:31.243 --> 00:08:33.012
<i> ( music playing )</i>

00:08:33.045 --> 00:08:36.649
<i> ( men shouting )</i>

00:08:36.682 --> 00:08:39.184
<i> As the country careened
 toward the Civil War,</i>

00:08:39.217 --> 00:08:41.820
<i> white people accused freed
 black people of insanity</i>

00:08:41.853 --> 00:08:45.057
<i> as a means to wield control.</i>

00:08:45.090 --> 00:08:49.295
<i> Samuel Cartwright,
 an assistant surgeon general
 of the Confederacy</i>

00:08:49.328 --> 00:08:53.299
<i> invented two mental disorders
 exclusively for black people</i>

00:08:53.332 --> 00:08:56.168
<i> to explain their tendencies
 to flee captivity--</i>

00:08:56.201 --> 00:09:00.005
<i> drapetomania
 and dysaethesia aethiopica.</i>

00:09:00.038 --> 00:09:04.777
<i> His cure was to
 "Whip the devil out of them."</i>

00:09:04.810 --> 00:09:07.680
<i> By the 20th century,
 psychiatrists had started</i>

00:09:07.713 --> 00:09:10.249 line:0%
<i> working on cures
 for mental illnesses.</i>

00:09:10.282 --> 00:09:13.319
<i> Through eugenics,
 the practice of improving
 the genetic composition</i>

00:09:13.352 --> 00:09:16.088
<i> of the human race
 through selective mating,</i>

00:09:16.121 --> 00:09:18.023
<i> the government forced
 the sterilization</i>

00:09:18.056 --> 00:09:21.827 line:0%
<i> of more than 60,000 supposed
 feeble-minded individuals.</i>

00:09:21.860 --> 00:09:24.196
<i> This included
 promiscuous women,</i>

00:09:24.229 --> 00:09:26.165
<i> immigrants,
 and people of color.</i>

00:09:26.198 --> 00:09:28.701 align:start position:0%
This one is wild.

00:09:28.734 --> 00:09:31.337 align:start position:0%
This is a Fittest Family
eugenics competition

00:09:31.370 --> 00:09:34.239 size:78% position:39% line:0%
at the Eugenics building?

00:09:34.272 --> 00:09:38.210 align:start position:0% line:0%
You can see sort of
the language of science
being used to justify

00:09:38.243 --> 00:09:42.047 align:start size:78% position:22%
whatever political action
comes next.

00:09:42.080 --> 00:09:45.951
 Fabiola:<i> By the middle
 of the 20th century,
 psychotropic drugs</i>

00:09:45.984 --> 00:09:50.656
<i> became the popular
 therapeutic treatment
 for mental illness.</i>

00:09:50.689 --> 00:09:53.225
<i> And following decades
 of overcrowding, neglect,</i>

00:09:53.258 --> 00:09:57.896
<i> and abuse in asylums,
 de-institutionalization
 began in 1955</i>

00:09:57.929 --> 00:09:59.798
<i> and resulted in the closure</i>

00:09:59.831 --> 00:10:03.068
<i> of state psychiatric
 hospitals and asylums.</i>

00:10:03.101 --> 00:10:06.672
<i> The re-entry of people
 with mental illnesses
 back into society</i>

00:10:06.705 --> 00:10:08.707
<i> foreshadowed
 the challenges they'd face</i>

00:10:08.740 --> 00:10:11.277
<i> in the criminal justice system
 decades later.</i>

00:10:14.146 --> 00:10:16.115
<i> ♪ Freedom ♪</i>

00:10:17.416 --> 00:10:19.184
<i> ♪ Oh, freedom ♪</i>

00:10:19.217 --> 00:10:21.253
Fabiola:<i> During the 1960s
 Civil Rights era,</i>

00:10:21.286 --> 00:10:23.922
<i> as black Americans
 led protests for equality,</i>

00:10:23.955 --> 00:10:27.259
<i> the American Psychiatric
 Association deliberately</i>

00:10:27.292 --> 00:10:31.997
<i> changed the definition
 of schizophrenia
 to include aggression.</i>

00:10:32.030 --> 00:10:34.933
<i> Prior to this,
 it was described
 as a largely harmless,</i>

00:10:34.966 --> 00:10:37.770
<i> docile condition
 exhibited by white patients.</i>

00:10:37.803 --> 00:10:42.241
<i> In 1968, two psychiatrists
 described schizophrenia</i>

00:10:42.274 --> 00:10:45.144
<i> as protest psychosis,
 which classified black people</i>

00:10:45.177 --> 00:10:47.079
<i> as hostile and aggressive.</i>

00:10:47.112 --> 00:10:51.984 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Protest psychosis?
  Holy ( bleep ).
- Wild.

00:10:52.017 --> 00:10:55.120
Fabiola:<i> In the years after,
 companies marketed
 pharmaceuticals</i>

00:10:55.153 --> 00:10:59.224
<i> directly to black patients
 whom they felt were
 out of control.</i>

00:10:59.257 --> 00:11:01.460 align:start size:59% position:41%
It's like these are
the modern versions

00:11:01.493 --> 00:11:03.228 align:start size:63% position:38%
of the diseases that
Cartwright invented.

00:11:03.261 --> 00:11:06.131 align:start position:0%
It doesn't even feel
like these ads are designed

00:11:06.164 --> 00:11:09.802 align:start position:0%
for people struggling
with schizophrenia.

00:11:09.835 --> 00:11:15.441 align:start position:0%
It feels like they are
designed for fear mongering.

00:11:15.474 --> 00:11:19.144
Fabiola:
<i> Deinstitutionalization
 and other social forces</i>

00:11:19.177 --> 00:11:22.081
<i> such as the criminalization
 of substance use</i>

00:11:22.114 --> 00:11:25.317 line:0%
<i> led to an overrepresentation
 of mentally ill people</i>

00:11:25.350 --> 00:11:27.753 line:0%
<i> in prisons and jails.</i>

00:11:27.786 --> 00:11:30.155 line:0%
Christophe:
I mean, if 37% of people
in state and federal prisons

00:11:30.188 --> 00:11:31.824 line:0%
have been told they've had
a mental illness,

00:11:31.857 --> 00:11:35.828 size:84% position:42% line:0%
it's like our prison system
is basically functioning

00:11:35.861 --> 00:11:39.998 align:start position:0%
as that system
of institutionalization
previously did,

00:11:40.031 --> 00:11:44.470 align:start position:0%
but just without
the intention of even
trying to be remediative

00:11:44.503 --> 00:11:47.707 size:84% position:42% line:0%
or a process of therapy.
It's just about punishment.

00:11:51.243 --> 00:11:52.778
Fabiola:
<i> You can now take off
 your headphones</i>

00:11:52.811 --> 00:11:54.880
<i> for the final portion
 of our exhibit.</i>

00:11:54.913 --> 00:11:56.148 align:start size:72% position:28%
- Hello.
- Cleo: Hello.

00:11:56.181 --> 00:11:57.850
So, I'm gonna walk you through

00:11:57.883 --> 00:12:00.152 align:start position:0%
the final portion
of the exhibit.

00:12:00.185 --> 00:12:02.988
If you can just
walk along here with me.

00:12:03.021 --> 00:12:05.023 align:start position:0%
Race has always
been intertwined

00:12:05.056 --> 00:12:09.027 align:start position:0%
with diagnoses and treatments
for mental illness,

00:12:09.060 --> 00:12:12.364 align:start position:0%
so if you're a person of color
categorized as mentally ill

00:12:12.397 --> 00:12:15.167 align:start position:0%
and you've never received
adequate treatment
or adequate care,

00:12:15.200 --> 00:12:17.970 align:start position:0%
that could be
a death sentence.

00:12:18.003 --> 00:12:20.773
Daniel Prude, Eleanor Bumpurs,

00:12:20.806 --> 00:12:23.408 line:0%
Walter Wallace Jr.,
Alfred Olango,

00:12:23.441 --> 00:12:26.979 line:0%
Angelo Quinto, and Ezell Ford.

00:12:27.012 --> 00:12:29.381 align:start position:0%
These are just a handful
of names of people

00:12:29.414 --> 00:12:31.350
killed at the hands of police

00:12:31.383 --> 00:12:34.987
when they needed
mental health services.

00:12:35.020 --> 00:12:38.223
So, to close out our exhibit,
I want to end with a statement

00:12:38.256 --> 00:12:41.059
from the American
Psychiatric Association.

00:12:41.092 --> 00:12:44.363
"Since the APA's inception,
practitioners have at times

00:12:44.396 --> 00:12:48.233
subjected persons
of African descent
and indigenous people

00:12:48.266 --> 00:12:51.403 line:0%
who suffer from mental illness
to abusive treatment,

00:12:51.436 --> 00:12:53.906 line:0%
experimentation, victimization,

00:12:53.939 --> 00:12:55.941 line:0%
in the name
of scientific evidence,

00:12:55.974 --> 00:13:00.078
along with racialized theories
that attempted to confirm

00:13:00.111 --> 00:13:02.080
their deficit status.

00:13:02.113 --> 00:13:03.982
These appalling past actions,

00:13:04.015 --> 00:13:05.551
as well as
their harmful effects,

00:13:05.584 --> 00:13:08.887
are ingrained in the structure
of psychiatric practice

00:13:08.920 --> 00:13:13.025 line:0%
and continue to harm
BIPOC's psychological
wellbeing even today."

00:13:13.058 --> 00:13:16.562
I feel like if we become
more aware of how systems

00:13:16.595 --> 00:13:19.431
of oppression operate
to disenfranchise people,

00:13:19.464 --> 00:13:24.436
that can be
sort of a back door

00:13:24.469 --> 00:13:27.973
to removing the stigma to things
that we think are not for us.

00:13:28.006 --> 00:13:33.111 line:0%
It's time to highlight those
marginalized communities

00:13:33.144 --> 00:13:35.948 line:0%
of therapy that we could
really use today,

00:13:35.981 --> 00:13:37.449
not only for ourselves,

00:13:37.482 --> 00:13:39.385
but I think
for the broader world.

00:13:40.919 --> 00:13:43.455
So, where do we go
when the systems

00:13:43.488 --> 00:13:46.325
that are supposed
to help us harm us?

00:13:46.358 --> 00:13:48.594
Fortunately,
there are therapists
who have been creating

00:13:48.627 --> 00:13:53.833
safe spaces to help us unpack
and heal from our traumas.

00:13:56.635 --> 00:13:59.605
Woman:
Now you can take in
a nice deep inhale.

00:13:59.638 --> 00:14:05.110 align:start position:0%
As I bathe you in sound,
just sit and take in
the vibration.

00:14:16.254 --> 00:14:18.991 align:start position:0%
Perhaps you can take in
a deep breath,

00:14:19.024 --> 00:14:21.226 size:81% position:41%
just to seal the practice.

00:14:21.259 --> 00:14:25.531
And when you're ready,
you're going to shift your gaze.

00:14:30.535 --> 00:14:32.437 align:start size:44% position:56%
Wow.

00:14:32.470 --> 00:14:35.340
- How was that for you?
- That was incredible.

00:14:35.373 --> 00:14:38.977 align:start size:69% position:31%
I feel like I struggle
to, like, calm my mind
and quiet my  mind.

00:14:39.010 --> 00:14:42.614 align:start size:78% position:22%
And I feel like it's
hard to ignore the sound,

00:14:42.647 --> 00:14:45.017 align:start size:78% position:22%
so this kind of helped me
focus in a way.

00:14:45.050 --> 00:14:46.919 align:start size:63% position:38%
It kind of felt
like I was escaping.

00:14:46.952 --> 00:14:49.354 align:start size:78% position:22%
Like, with each new sound
that you introduced,

00:14:49.387 --> 00:14:52.658 align:start size:88% position:13%
it was like it
opened up this level of,
I don't know, consciousness.

00:14:52.691 --> 00:14:56.662 align:start position:0% line:0%
This is a part of how
therapy can be unconventional,

00:14:56.695 --> 00:15:01.366 align:start position:0% line:0%
and how it can actually
bring in some of these
indigenous practices

00:15:01.399 --> 00:15:05.537 align:start position:0%
that we know have been
really profoundly healing

00:15:05.570 --> 00:15:07.673 align:start position:0%
for our communities
for generations.

00:15:07.706 --> 00:15:11.076 align:start position:0%
It integrates that experience
that is more holistic.

00:15:11.109 --> 00:15:13.378 align:start position:0%
So, one question
that I like to ask everyone

00:15:13.411 --> 00:15:16.448 align:start position:0%
whenever we're completing
the practice is,
how's your soul?

00:15:16.481 --> 00:15:18.483 align:start size:28% position:72%
Ooh.

00:15:18.516 --> 00:15:20.285 align:start size:56% position:44%
I don't even know
how to answer that

00:15:20.318 --> 00:15:22.220 align:start size:63% position:38%
because I feel like
I'm always concerned

00:15:22.253 --> 00:15:25.023 align:start size:81% position:19%
with how my mind is doing,

00:15:25.056 --> 00:15:28.026
that I don't really question
what's going on with my soul.

00:15:28.059 --> 00:15:29.995 align:start size:50% position:50%
I feel grounded,
in a sense,

00:15:30.028 --> 00:15:34.399 align:start size:63% position:38%
but not completely,
like, at peace,
if that makes sense.

00:15:34.432 --> 00:15:38.070 align:start position:0%
Absolutely.
The racial epidemic,
the pandemic,

00:15:38.103 --> 00:15:41.406 align:start position:0%
and being a person that
lies at the intersection

00:15:41.439 --> 00:15:45.410 align:start position:0%
of multiple identities
that are marginalized
and vulnerable

00:15:45.443 --> 00:15:48.246 align:start position:0%
really makes it
so that ease is something

00:15:48.279 --> 00:15:51.283 align:start position:0%
that is a little bit
more challenging

00:15:51.316 --> 00:15:53.085
for you to be able to acquire.

00:15:53.118 --> 00:15:57.723 align:start position:0%
It's important to offset stress
with self-care practices--

00:15:57.756 --> 00:16:00.993 align:start position:0%
whatever healing
looks like for you,
for you to emphasize that.

00:16:01.026 --> 00:16:05.330 align:start position:0%
There's so many ways that
healing can be introduced
into our lives

00:16:05.363 --> 00:16:07.432 align:start position:0%
that steps outside
of the 45 minutes

00:16:07.465 --> 00:16:10.435 align:start position:0%
that you have
in a therapeutic space.

00:16:10.468 --> 00:16:12.437
Therapy could be as simple

00:16:12.470 --> 00:16:16.008
as walking,
you know, in a forest,

00:16:16.041 --> 00:16:20.445
and energy work, like,
reiki-type of work as well.

00:16:20.478 --> 00:16:22.581
You know, I've done a little
bit of yoga in the past.

00:16:22.614 --> 00:16:24.783
But, like, that--
it's just not my thing.

00:16:24.816 --> 00:16:29.287
I just-- it feels like
I'm too aware of my body
around everybody else.

00:16:29.320 --> 00:16:33.759 line:0%
I think everybody's
open to some level of
holistic health,

00:16:33.792 --> 00:16:36.628 line:0%
whether that be yoga,
meditation, like,

00:16:36.661 --> 00:16:39.464 line:0%
even essential oils,
and, like, you know,

00:16:39.497 --> 00:16:42.467 line:0%
just the idea of self-care.

00:16:42.500 --> 00:16:47.139
Humor, like,
just being able to laugh
once or twice a day,

00:16:47.172 --> 00:16:50.509
like, really a good belly laugh
helps me tremendously,

00:16:50.542 --> 00:16:53.812
as well as a really,
really good cry.

00:16:53.845 --> 00:16:55.580
Fabiola:
Different forms of therapy,

00:16:55.613 --> 00:16:58.517
whether they support
talk therapy or supplant it,

00:16:58.550 --> 00:17:00.519
are finding their way out
to the mainstream.

00:17:00.552 --> 00:17:03.488 line:0%
<i> I'm encouraged
 by the mental health
 advocacy movement</i>

00:17:03.521 --> 00:17:06.158 line:0%
<i> on platforms like Instagram
 that are encouraging people</i>

00:17:06.191 --> 00:17:07.793 line:0%
<i> to prioritize
 their mental health</i>

00:17:07.826 --> 00:17:10.162 line:0%
<i> and figure out
 the kind of therapy</i>

00:17:10.195 --> 00:17:11.630 line:0%
<i> that works for them.</i>

00:17:11.663 --> 00:17:13.799
As for me, I took
Dr. Buqué's advice to heart.

00:17:13.832 --> 00:17:16.301
I'm on my way to spend
some time in nature.

00:17:16.334 --> 00:17:18.236
<i> I had a number of options
 to choose from,</i>

00:17:18.269 --> 00:17:20.605
<i> but landed on equine therapy</i>

00:17:20.638 --> 00:17:22.374
<i> because I wanted to think
 more about the connection</i>

00:17:22.407 --> 00:17:24.509
<i> between my mind and body</i>

00:17:24.542 --> 00:17:26.845
<i> and how trauma manifests
 physically.</i>

00:17:26.878 --> 00:17:31.316
<i> I'm gonna be doing therapeutic
 riding at GallopNYC.</i>

00:17:31.349 --> 00:17:32.484
I'm really excited.

00:17:37.555 --> 00:17:40.092
Man:
<i> When you're working
 with a horse,</i>

00:17:40.125 --> 00:17:41.793
<i> you have to be very in tune
 to what's happening
 on their side, right?</i>

00:17:41.826 --> 00:17:43.829 line:0%
So, if I come in
and I steamroll you

00:17:43.862 --> 00:17:47.199 line:0%
with my opinions
and my feelings and-- it's just
not gonna work, right?

00:17:47.232 --> 00:17:49.301
Because the horse
has his own feelings,
his own opinions,

00:17:49.334 --> 00:17:51.503
<i> so I have to partner
 with him.</i>

00:17:51.536 --> 00:17:55.140
<i> It doesn't matter
 the color of your skin,
 how you walk in here.</i>

00:17:55.173 --> 00:17:57.742
<i> None of those things
 matter to a horse.</i>

00:17:57.775 --> 00:18:00.212 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
Being on a horse,
you put somebody on a horse,

00:18:00.245 --> 00:18:02.781 align:start size:75% position:25% line:0%
a kid on a horse,
that kid is more relaxed

00:18:02.814 --> 00:18:04.316
<i> because it's something new.</i>

00:18:04.349 --> 00:18:06.151
<i> Never really had
 that experience.</i>

00:18:06.184 --> 00:18:08.520 align:start size:53% position:47% line:0%
Fabiola:
So, how do horses
aid in therapy?

00:18:08.553 --> 00:18:11.756 align:start position:0%
Payne:
Horses can really
feel your energy.

00:18:11.789 --> 00:18:15.427 align:start position:0%
Just their movement
is really helpful
for the rider.

00:18:15.460 --> 00:18:18.430 align:start position:0%
It helps them really
to relax, you know?

00:18:18.463 --> 00:18:21.733 size:75% position:38%
She's just coming around
to smell you and say hi.

00:18:21.766 --> 00:18:24.736 size:81% position:41% line:0%
She appreciates the groom.

00:18:31.276 --> 00:18:32.511
Fabiola:
Walk on, Sadie.

00:18:35.346 --> 00:18:38.617 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Whoa, back.
- And release. Good.

00:18:38.650 --> 00:18:43.255 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Walk on, Sadie. Yay!
- Excellent.

00:18:43.288 --> 00:18:44.556 align:start position:0%
Very nice.

00:18:44.589 --> 00:18:47.425 align:start size:75% position:25%
- Trot on.
- There you go.

00:18:47.458 --> 00:18:49.895 align:start size:84% position:16%
So nice, firm.
A little bit more pressure.

00:18:49.928 --> 00:18:51.897 align:start size:75% position:25%
- There you go.
- Nice.

00:18:54.199 --> 00:18:57.369
- And we can walk.
- Walk on, Sadie.

00:18:57.402 --> 00:18:59.171 align:start position:0%
Okay, we'll take
a quiet lap around

00:18:59.204 --> 00:19:01.273 align:start position:0%
so you can
process and relax.

00:19:13.484 --> 00:19:14.920
Fabiola:
<i> Just feeling calm right now,</i>

00:19:14.953 --> 00:19:18.290
<i> feeling just relaxed.</i>

00:19:20.391 --> 00:19:22.294
<i> Listening to Sadie
 and understanding</i>

00:19:22.327 --> 00:19:26.198
<i> how my different
 hand placements</i>

00:19:26.231 --> 00:19:28.967
<i> affect her and direct her.</i>

00:19:29.000 --> 00:19:32.604
<i> That, for me,
 really is about focus</i>

00:19:32.637 --> 00:19:35.340
<i> and getting myself
 to remember</i>

00:19:35.373 --> 00:19:37.342
<i> to be present and mindful.</i>

00:19:39.244 --> 00:19:43.315 align:start position:0%
So, before you get off, I want
you to give her some pats.

00:19:43.348 --> 00:19:46.651 align:start position:0%
We like to tell them
that they did good for us.

00:19:46.684 --> 00:19:49.321 align:start position:0%
- Sadie did such an amazing job.
- Especially if it's true.

00:19:49.354 --> 00:19:51.556 align:start size:69% position:31%
I feel so happy.
I kind of want to cry.

00:19:51.589 --> 00:19:54.326 align:start size:75% position:25%
She's-- like, seriously,
this was really sweet.

00:19:54.359 --> 00:19:56.328 align:start position:0%
Oh, I'm glad you had
such a good experience.

00:19:56.361 --> 00:19:59.998 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Yeah, this was great.
- It's okay.

00:20:00.031 --> 00:20:02.968
I feel like such a crybaby,
but I really did feel,

00:20:03.001 --> 00:20:04.970
yeah, just a connection,
and she's just awesome.

00:20:05.003 --> 00:20:08.673 align:start position:0%
And this is really
what happens with
the riders every day.

00:20:08.706 --> 00:20:11.676 align:start size:78% position:22%
Good, and then you slide.
There you go.

00:20:11.709 --> 00:20:14.512 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Thank you so much.
- You're welcome.

00:20:14.545 --> 00:20:17.282
Thank you, Sadie.

00:20:17.315 --> 00:20:18.650
<i> I didn't expect to cry</i>

00:20:18.683 --> 00:20:20.518
<i> at the end of my time
 with Sadie,</i>

00:20:20.551 --> 00:20:22.854
but I feel like I got
so much from that experience,

00:20:22.887 --> 00:20:25.357
and I fully understand
how therapy can help us

00:20:25.390 --> 00:20:28.426
improve our functionality
and quality of life.

00:20:28.459 --> 00:20:31.363
But so many people
can't access therapy.

00:20:31.396 --> 00:20:34.532
I think black people,
whether we know it or not,

00:20:34.565 --> 00:20:36.534
the pain of our ancestors
lives through our body

00:20:36.567 --> 00:20:38.770
as much as the beauty
and the joy.

00:20:38.803 --> 00:20:40.705
White-dominant society
in general

00:20:40.738 --> 00:20:43.875
forces us to be stronger
than we need to be

00:20:43.908 --> 00:20:45.510
in a lot of situations.

00:20:45.543 --> 00:20:47.879
We're in this moment
in American history

00:20:47.912 --> 00:20:51.016
where we're just scratching
the surface of, like,

00:20:51.049 --> 00:20:55.887
coming to under--
or acknowledge all of the--

00:20:55.920 --> 00:20:57.722
the trauma that this country

00:20:57.755 --> 00:20:59.557
has inflicted
on people of color.

00:20:59.590 --> 00:21:02.027
Like, the least you can do,
if it's not reparations,

00:21:02.060 --> 00:21:04.296
at least give us free therapy.

00:21:04.329 --> 00:21:06.898 align:start size:91% position:9%
- Do I think therapy
  is for everyone?
- Is therapy for everyone?

00:21:06.931 --> 00:21:08.867
I don't think therapy's
for everybody.

00:21:08.900 --> 00:21:10.902
Yes, therapy is for everyone.

00:21:10.935 --> 00:21:14.039
I think that there is a type
of therapy for everyone.

00:21:14.072 --> 00:21:17.575
If we could reshape
the medical industrial complex,

00:21:17.608 --> 00:21:19.411
then it could be for everyone.

00:21:19.444 --> 00:21:22.047
Fabiola:
<i> But we need to, as a nation,</i>

00:21:22.080 --> 00:21:23.982
<i> recognize that mental health</i>

00:21:24.015 --> 00:21:26.384
<i> is just as important
 as our physical health.</i>

00:21:26.417 --> 00:21:29.587
Expanding access
to different kinds of therapy

00:21:29.620 --> 00:21:32.357 line:0%
can help more people
find peace.

00:21:32.390 --> 00:21:34.392 align:start size:63% position:38% line:0%
So, what else
are you noticing?

00:21:34.425 --> 00:21:36.961 line:0%
I feel like I don't know
what I'm doing.

00:21:36.994 --> 00:21:38.697 align:start size:81% position:19% line:0%
- Let me start over.
- Pew!

00:21:38.730 --> 00:21:40.031 align:start size:72% position:28% line:0%
Everyone does this,
by the way.

00:21:40.064 --> 00:21:41.666 size:84% position:58% line:0%
It's not because I'm short.

00:21:41.699 --> 00:21:45.670 line:0%
- I'm glad you asked.
- I'm glad you asked.

00:21:45.703 --> 00:21:47.306 line:0%
See what we did there?

